 "result": "**Mechanism:** Werkzeug `LocalProxy` objects backed by a single `ContextVar`. Each proxy looks up the current value of the context variable on every access and then reads an attribute from it.\n\n**Where:** `src/tessel/globals.py`\n\n- `_app_var` (line 40) is a `ContextVar[AppContext]` named `\"tessel.app_ctx\"`. It holds the current `AppContext`, which is pushed and popped by `app.app_context()` or a request.\n- `active_app` (line 44) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"app\")`, which resolves to the `Tessel` application (`.app` on the context).\n- `g` (line 47) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"g\")`, which resolves to the `_AppCtxGlobals` object (`.g` on the context).\n- `request` (line 57) is `LocalProxy(_app_var, \"request\")`, which resolves to the `Request` object (`.request` on the context).\n\n`app_ctx` (line 41) is the proxy for the context itself, and `session` (line 60) works the same way as the others. The context classes are `AppContext` and `_AppCtxGlobals` in `src/tessel/ctx.py`.\n\nIf no context is pushed, the `ContextVar` is unset and the proxy raises a `RuntimeError` with the \"outside of application context\" message. For `request` and `session` the message says \"outside of request context\" instead. The `request` and `session` proxies also read from `_app_var`, because the request context has been merged into the app context. The old `request_ctx` name now just returns `app_ctx` with a deprecation warning.",
